# Open: what can move. Questions (6.1), assumptions the design stands on (6.2), limits of the evidence (6.3).

n: 6.1
name: questions
kind: hub
is: The open questions. None blocks the node encoding or the floor; each can be settled by building and measuring, or by the guide's word.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 6.1.2
name: one-or-node
kind: question
is: Is an ordinary returned value a 1 that carries a payload, or a node? And is a node as acknowledgment a result, a counter-offer or a finding? The ground documents give three readings.
status: open
see: 3.3.4.10 5.4.2
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.29
name: edges-of-the-three-states
kind: question
is: Within the guide's definition of ice, water and gas: may something derivable be pinned as ice, is water one state or two (a read-only cache and a changeable working copy), and does gas have a body or only a recipe that can derive it?
status: open
see: 5.6.2 3.14
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.30
name: what-counts-as-a-second-hand
kind: question
is: What makes a reader of the encoding a second hand? New code is written in the grammar and kept as nodes (7.13), so a second reader runs on the first hand's walker and store. Is it a second hand when another session of the same model writes it from the page alone, or must a person or another model write it? The same answer decides who may write a review under hub 9: so far no claim is reviewed.
status: open
see: 7.2 7.13 8.8
at: 2026-11-25

n: 6.1.31
name: who-asked-for-sha-256
kind: question
is: The guide asked on 2026-11-25: 'Who asked for sha256?' Nobody did. 5.2.2 chose it from a timing and a collision estimate, and the encoding page, the ice file and the vectors follow it. What should a node's id be, and must it be a digest? An id that needs operations on bits also asks them of every reader written in form.
status: open
see: 5.2.2 6.1.4 6.1.18 7.2
at: 2026-11-25

n: 6.1.4
name: id-shape
kind: question
is: Is a node's id a digest, a coordinate, or a handle onto a digest? If the four words package, level, type and instance stay, what does each mean? NUMS.Go reads level as tree height and the old runtime reads it as the category's level.
status: open
see: 5.2.6 5.2.1 3.1.8.1 3.4.2.2
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.5
name: literals-in-identity
kind: question
is: Are literal values part of a recipe's identity? NUMS.Go keeps them outside the tree; the proposal in 5.2.5 puts them inside and derives a value-free shape beside.
status: open
see: 5.2.5 3.1.3.3 3.1.4.2
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.6
name: nominal-or-structural
kind: question
is: Do two blueprints with the same fields in the same order share one identity? Are field names part of it? NUMS.Go as built gives same-shape structs different ids and keeps field names outside identity; neither consequence was chosen on purpose.
status: open
see: 3.8 3.1.2.7 5.2.4
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.7
name: default-choice
kind: question
is: Is the default choice re-entrant, as in the 2000 sources, or committed at exit, as in all 2026 code? What is the scope of cut? A re-entrant default keeps every answered choice alive as a recover point, and the cost of that has not been measured.
status: open
see: 5.4.7 3.5.2.5
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.9
name: breach-prevented-or-observed
kind: question
is: Does the kernel prevent a crossing outside an offered interface, or only record it? The axiom asks only that breach be observable.
status: open
see: 1.9.4 5.8.4
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.10
name: seed-form
kind: question
is: What does the repository hold for a cold start: text in the home lens read by a larger C reader, a canonical-print seed regenerated by the system, or both as in 5.9.5? Who writes the first seed, and is there a second independent reader to check it?
status: open
see: 5.9.5 5.3.4 3.6.4.3
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.11
name: integer-model
kind: question
is: What is the integer law: fixed 64-bit, arbitrary precision, or width carried by the leaf's blueprint? How are machine-level blueprints written (width, signedness, byte layout), which any emitter needs before it can exist? The old 63-bit wrapping integer was an artifact of tagging.
status: open
see: 5.1.4 3.4.2.6 3.5.8.8
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.12
name: concurrency-unit
kind: question
is: What is the unit of concurrency? Nothing read here covers it, and the per-thread switch for writing code constrains which thread may emit.
status: open
see: 5.8.6 5.9.4
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.13
name: does-the-walker-stay
kind: question
is: After the compiler is compiled, does the walker stay in the process as a fallback, or leave so that no cold and hot pair exists? Is one small independent reader of the node encoding worth keeping as a second witness? The old body's best bug findings and its largest costs both came from second implementations.
status: open
see: 5.10.3 3.7.4.6 3.7.2.1
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.14
name: cycles-in-a-hashed-store
kind: question
is: How do mutually recursive definitions get content ids when a store of hashed nodes cannot contain a cycle? Unison hashes the cycle as one unit; that was recalled, not verified.
status: open
see: 5.2.2 5.7.1 4.5.3.1
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.15
name: effects-that-left
kind: question
is: Inside an open choice, is an effect that leaves the system forbidden, buffered until commit, or paired with a compensating recipe? Nobody read has solved this; each pushes it to an edge and names it.
status: open
see: 5.4.8 4.5.1.4
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.16
name: store-in-the-request
kind: question
is: In the request's list 'choice, fail, stop, store', does store mean a checkpoint to return to, or persistence? In the dialect the old body actually runs, store and restore are identity functions over immutable values.
status: open
see: 3.5.2.7
needs: guide
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.17
name: home-lens-syntax
kind: question
is: What does the home lens look like on the page? Constraints so far: it must carry choice, commit, classes with several parents, templates and attributes, and every rule must be printable by running its template backwards.
status: open
see: 5.3.5 5.3.2
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.18
name: collision-resistance-inside
kind: question
is: Does identity inside one process on one machine need resistance to deliberate collisions, or only identity that crosses a wire? This decides between storing 128 and 256 bits.
status: open
see: 5.2.2 3.15
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.19
name: first-human-language-lens
kind: question
is: What counts as success for the first human-language lens, and which inventory of concepts does it stand on? A candidate: one set of abstract constructions, two tongues with different word order and one with agreement, linearisation as data, every miss a fault, and a round trip checked by a test shown able to fail.
status: open
see: 5.3.7 3.10
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.20
name: executable-nums
kind: question
is: Does an executable NUMS exist anywhere, such as the C++ original the Go code was ported from? Neither NUMS.Go nor the 2026 miniature evaluates anything, and no C++ source is on this disk.
status: open
see: 3.1.1.8 3.8
needs: guide
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.21
name: fault-at-a-recover-point
kind: question
is: How does a recover point see a fault: as a node acknowledgment whose blueprint is 'fault', or as a separate channel? And how are 'no refusals' and 'no silent recovery', both asked for by the guide, met together?
status: open
see: 5.4.5 3.7.3.3
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.22
name: branching-on-nothing
kind: question
is: What does a conditional do when its test acknowledges nothing: raise a fault, take a third arm, or count as a failed option? Is every non-zero value true?
status: open
see: 3.4.1.1 5.4.1
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.23
name: speculative-tier-shape
kind: question
is: Is the speculative tier trace-based or method-based, and does a mostly pure space of content-addressed nodes make a sea-of-nodes form favourable again?
status: open
see: 5.10.6 4.2.2.1 4.2.2.3
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.24
name: held-work-at-the-leaf
kind: question
is: Is held work, an offer that has not yet arrived, visible to machine-code leaves? The 2026-11-05 carrier keeps it outside the acknowledgment word and collapses it to nothing at the leaf boundary.
status: open
see: 3.13 3.3.4.8 1.7.17
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.25
name: are-live-nodes-interned
kind: question
is: Are live, unfrozen nodes interned so that equal content is one node in memory, or is equality by content found only at freeze? Measured cost of interning with a cheap hash and byte comparison: 14 to 25 ns and 56 bytes per node.
status: open
see: 5.2.3 3.15
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.2
name: assumptions
kind: hub
is: Seven things the design takes as true without proof. The heaviest: that a walker of about 25 operations can run the compiler once, and that 2,000 lines of C are enough.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.2.1
name: first-machine
kind: assumption
is: The first target is this Apple silicon Mac (M4 Max, macOS, AArch64 with matrix instructions), then Linux on AArch64 and x86-64, then Windows.
status: assumed
see: 4.2.4.1
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.2.2
name: small-walker-suffices
kind: assumption
is: A walker over about 25 core operations is enough to run the compiler once on a cold start, and does not need to be fast.
status: assumed
see: 5.9.3 3.13
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.2.3
name: lens-rows-scale
kind: assumption
is: Lens rows read forwards and printed backwards keep working, at a cost near linear, beyond the 15 constructs the probe covered: declarations, generics, pattern matching, modules, layout by indentation.
status: assumed
see: 5.3.2 3.11
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.2.4
name: delegation-and-undo-compose
kind: assumption
is: A reference of cell and view composes with backtracking once the whole reference is saved on every choice point. With parents composed into one shape there is no second object to restore; no run yet shows a view change undone.
status: assumed
see: 5.5.2 3.12
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.2.5
name: canonical-print-stays-a-serialization
kind: assumption
is: The canonical print does not grow into a second dialect, because nobody writes in it after the first seed and it has no forms of its own. The old body's split began the same way.
status: assumed
see: 5.3.4 3.4.9.1
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.2.6
name: budget-is-enough
kind: assumption
is: 2,000 lines of C hold everything in 5.9.3. Four estimates stand between 1,350 and 2,200; none has been built.
status: assumed
see: 5.9.2 3.13 3.6.4.3
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.2.7
name: references-inform-nothing-is-imported
kind: assumption
is: NUMS.Go, the old body and the 2000 sources are read for what they show and nothing is carried over as code, except the roughly 460 lines of the 2026-11-05 carrier that match this design nearly unchanged.
status: assumed
see: 3.13 5.9.8
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.3
name: limits
kind: hub
is: Limits of the evidence and of the floor. NUMS.Go executes nothing and the object model's hardest case has no recorded run. Web claims came through a summarising tool, and no claim is confirmed by an independent reader.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 6.3.1
name: nums-executes-nothing
kind: limit
is: NUMS.Go represents a subset of Go as numbers and prints part of it back. It evaluates nothing, does not build as checked in, and in a patched copy stops on its own three sample files. It demonstrates names as a detachable lens; it is not a working executable numeric space.
status: inferred
from: 3.1.1.2 3.1.1.3 3.1.1.5 3.1.1.8 3.2.1.1 3.2.4.1 3.8.2.4 3.1.3.2
conf: high
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.3.2
name: object-model-records
kind: limit
is: The reference pair has run logs from the 2000 machine. Separate base objects with self kept while this moves have no recorded execution in 2000 or 2026; a 2026 fixture for a delegated method reaching an override stood for two weeks and was deleted on 2026-09-29. What the 2026 code calls delegation is flattening into one record.
status: inferred
from: 3.12.4.1 3.12.4.2 3.12.4.8 3.12.4.4 3.12.4.5 3.12.2.4 3.5.3.3 3.5.3.5
conf: high
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.3.3
name: bml-is-several-things
kind: limit
is: BML in 2000 is four technologies by two authors with unfinished sections; in 2026 three readers give its control words different meanings. In the dialect nearly all 2026 code uses, fail is the value nothing and store and restore are identity functions. No single BML exists to be the reference.
status: inferred
from: 3.5.1.1 3.5.1.2 3.5.1.3 3.5.2.4 3.5.2.7
conf: high
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.3.4
name: human-language-evidence
kind: limit
is: The old body's evidence that tongues share a root is one sentence shape in two tongues with about 50 words each, joined by a hand-made table; the check of the north star's own example sentence was deleted on 2026-09-29.
status: inferred
from: 3.10.1.8 3.10.2.1 3.10.2.6
conf: high
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.3.5
name: shared-identity-across-languages
kind: limit
is: A test that one method read from Go, Java and the home language becomes one node existed until 2026-09-29, and agreed by reading types and not keeping them. A probe shows it again for about 15 constructs, with a lexicon row that treats three integer types as one.
status: inferred
from: 3.11.3.2 3.11.4.4 3.11.4.10 3.11.9.6
conf: high
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.3.6
name: web-claims-are-relayed
kind: limit
is: Every claim from the web reached its reader through a fetch tool that summarises pages, and they were checked with the same tool. Short quotations and numbers from papers should be read at the source before anything is built on them.
status: inferred
from: 4.5.4 4.3.3.6
conf: high
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.3.7
name: records-not-runs
kind: limit
is: Probes were run on the old runtime for only a few claims. Claims about what the old body does rest mostly on its sources, its notes and recorded numbers, some dated before later faults.
status: inferred
from: 3.5.1.4 3.10 3.12
conf: high
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.3.8
name: one-noisy-machine
kind: limit
is: Every measurement was taken on one shared machine with other work running; one hash timing was 2.3 times off until a clock probe was added. The figures are minimums on that host.
status: inferred
from: 3.15.1.2 3.15.1.3
conf: high
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.3.9
name: open-fault-in-the-old-emitter-path
kind: limit
is: A fault recorded on 2026-11-24, a closure over locals going wrong at about its 1,024th call, has no known root cause. The threshold is the C fast path's, so nothing of that path is borrowed.
status: inferred
from: 3.7.2.3 3.9.5.5 3.9.5.3
conf: mid
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.3.10
name: evidence-is-unreviewed
kind: limit
is: The claims of hubs 3 and 4 are written from readings of their sources, and none is confirmed by an independent reader. A claim marked assumed has no source in this repository. A claim is confirmed only where hub 9 holds a review of its present digest; './kg check' prints the count.
status: observed
src: 2.30.1
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.26
name: unseen-node-choice
kind: question
is: Can a frozen model, through one shared out-projector, choose a node it never saw in fitting, at a rate that makes node rows useful? What width, and how are rows normalised so new ones neither steal the distribution nor never win?
status: open
see: 5.12.3 3.16.9
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.27
name: which-layers-can-learn
kind: question
is: Learned input rows and adapters on earlier layers need a backward pass through every layer; for the 27B's recurrent layers that pass is unwritten. Is it written, or are closed-form fits on captured activations enough?
status: open
see: 3.16.8 3.16.7
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.1.28
name: model-as-cell
kind: question
is: What interface does a model offer as a cell: which hook points, which state (cached attention, recurrent state) can be snapshotted as a node, and what does another model with other shapes need so the same node rows serve it?
status: open
see: 5.12.9 3.16.16
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.3.11
name: model-hub-is-arithmetic
kind: limit
is: Hub 5.12 rests on arithmetic over one model's shapes, two small probes on a noisy machine, notes of the old body, and published results read through a summarising tool. No part of it is built.
status: inferred
from: 3.16.3 3.16.4 3.16.5
conf: high
at: 2026-11-24

n: 6.3.12
name: a-frame-is-a-sample
kind: limit
is: A recording holds frames taken at intervals. What a record held between two frames is not kept, apart from its last writer, and a frame taken while the writer runs can join parts from slightly different moments; it is marked as moving. Old values will be kept inside the region only when the journal that undo needs is built in stage 5.
status: inferred
from: 5.7.8 5.14.10
conf: high
at: 2026-11-25

n: 6.3.13
name: free-recording-is-not-a-free-layout
kind: limit
is: The guide's definition asks for recording with no impact at all. The floor adds no instruction to the runtime for a reader, and two costs remain. A reader copying pages every millisecond slowed the writer by up to about 4% through shared cache lines. The walker keeps its registers and each record's writer in memory, which cost it about a quarter of its speed on one program (5.14.13); whether compiled code can keep that near zero is not known.
status: inferred
from: 5.14.13 5.7.6
conf: mid
at: 2026-11-25

